  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10e-catfishes-from-asia/hara_jerdoni_en/",
      "html": "<p>Hara jerdoni is a tiny moth catfish that attains only a length of 2-3 cm and has very, very long pectoral fin spines. In some swimming positions it looks like a passenger airplane.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10e-catfishes-from-asia/hara_jerdoni_en/",
      "html": "<p>Moth cats (Hara) occur in India, Burma, and Thailand. The species are very difficult to tell apart, in many cases it is simply impossible in live animals. There are a few species that can be determined by a filament on the caudal fin, but most of the species are extremely similar looking. Hara jerdoni is an exception to this rule.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10e-catfishes-from-asia/hara_jerdoni_en/",
      "html": "<p>Since Hara in general do not swim a lot, all species can be kept in small tanks.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10e-catfishes-from-asia/hara_jerdoni_en/",
      "html": "<p>Moth catfish are very peaceful towards conspecifics and other fish.</p>"
    }
  ],
